{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790262080,"nonce":"4+lwkL/r6ngZK3hhipgdOXK6lrI=","title":"Python: `in` on an instance without __contains__ does not iterate it, and crashes the interpreter tier on arm64","message":"class Bag(object):\n        def __init__(self, items): self.items = items\n        def __iter__(self): return iter(self.items)\n    print(2 in Bag([1, 2]))\n\nCPython prints True. zypy on main dea6d08b (NUC, release) raises TypeError 'Bag' object is not iterable: membership on an instance goes through zb_any_iter, which does not reach the class's __iter__. For a class with neither __contains__ nor __iter__, CPython raises TypeError argument of type 'Box' is not iterable; zypy raises 'Box' object is not iterable on the NUC, and on the Mac a debug build (main of 2026-09-19 as well) dies with EXC_BAD_ACCESS in hir_interp read_typed: zb_list_contains_any reads the null list zb_any_iter hands back after raising.\n\nMembership now falls back to zb_any_contains_iter for such classes (both the default hook and the Python hook), so fixing zb_any_iter for instances and checking its raise there covers both.","files":null}]}